User story one: Enforcing daily card transaction ceilings

As an account holder I want the banking system to stop withdrawals or transfers that exceed my debit card tier limits
So that my funds remain protected against fraud and accidental overdrafts beyond my daily allowance.

acceptance criteria:

GIVEN that a customer holds an active account with a standard or tiered debit card
WHEN they attempt to withdraw an amount where the new amount
plus today's previous withdrawals exceeds the card tier's daily withdrawal limit
THEN the transaction is declined immediately and an error message
 displays stating the daily limit and amount already used today.

GIVEN that a customer initiates an internal transfer
between their own accounts
WHEN the requested transfer amount plus previous own-transfers
 is within the card's specific own-transfer ceiling
THEN the system approves the transaction, updates both account
 balances, and logs the operation.

GIVEN that a customer has reached their daily
 card transfer limit to external accounts
WHEN they attempt to initiate another transfer
to a third-party account on the same day
THEN the system blocks the request and prevents
funds from being deducted from the source account.



User story two: Automatic checking account overdraft fee and penalty lockout

As a banker I want checking accounts to automatically assess
 fees on negative balances and deactivate when hitting the overdraft limit
So that the bank mitigates credit risk while enforcing compliance rules systematically.

acceptance criteria:

GIVEN that a checking account has a positive balance but insufficient funds for
a requested withdrawal within the overdraft cap
WHEN the withdrawal executes
THEN the account balance becomes negative, the
 overdraft counter increases by one, and a fixed overdraft fee is deducted.

GIVEN that a checking account incurs its maximum allowed
 consecutive overdraft penalty
WHEN the threshold is reached
THEN the account status changes to DEACTIVATED
and all subsequent withdrawal requests are blocked.

GIVEN that a checking account is currently deactivated
 due to a negative balance
WHEN a deposit is processed that brings the account
balance back to zero or higher
THEN the system automatically reactivates the account
 to ACTIVE status and allows normal transactions to resume.